  {
    "@id": "ArtifactNode/00C5H15NT0000",
    "@type": "ArtifactNode",
    "text": "`Aperas-core-ontology-design.md` §5.A names three projection modes that turn a stored\n`BlockNode` tree back into an output: Artifact Projection (serialize back to the physical\n`.md` file, always fully unfolded regardless of each block's `unfolded` state), the Human UI,\nand the Agentic BFS interface. This doc settles the design for the first of the three —\nArtifact Projection, the direct inverse of `astParser.ts`'s Markdown → tree transform. The\nother two modes are out of scope here. The design below is settled; nothing has been\nimplemented yet.",
    "title": "Aperas-artifact-projection-design.md",
    "artifactId": "00C5H15NT0000",
    "fileHash": "17fc83bc70d013c020b5e1e69c979a124ed99b457caf5f710dcb7bbae9ddda0c",
    "ingestedHash": "17fc83bc70d013c020b5e1e69c979a124ed99b457caf5f710dcb7bbae9ddda0c",
    "lastIngestedAt": "2026-08-30T02:02:21.410Z",
    "lastTrackedAt": "2026-08-30T02:02:17.169Z",
    "path": "Aperas-artifact-projection-design.md",
    "root": "BlockNode/00C64YWTRG000"
  },
  {
    "@id": "ArtifactNode/00C66H6T9G000",
    "@type": "ArtifactNode",
    "text": "The original roadmap (`Aperas-design.md`'s Development Roadmap) only ever named one assertion-\nrelated deliverable — Phase 2's \"graph search skill\" — and treated the Human UI (Phase 1) as\nthe thing that lets humans/agents see what's in the graph at all. Neither assumption survived\nPhase 0 finishing:",
    "title": "Aperas-basic-assertion-skill-design.md",
    "artifactId": "00C66H6T9G000",
    "fileHash": "20332acc4c9cec651d11785a2c8018a5ccef052aec86f41461696297d5718be7",
    "ingestedHash": "20332acc4c9cec651d11785a2c8018a5ccef052aec86f41461696297d5718be7",
    "lastIngestedAt": "2026-08-30T04:57:26.702Z",
    "lastTrackedAt": "2026-08-30T04:57:23.590Z",
    "path": "Aperas-basic-assertion-skill-design.md",
    "root": "BlockNode/00C66H7K7G000"
  },
  {
    "@id": "ArtifactNode/00C66ZNX2R000",
    "@type": "ArtifactNode",
    "text": "`Aperas-core-ontology-design.md` §5.A names three projection modes. Mode 1 (Artifact\nProjection) is built (`Aperas-artifact-projection-design.md`). Mode 3 — *\"Agentic Interface\n(BFS Traversal): AI agents interact with the graph exactly like the Human UI... receive folded\nviews and can navigate continuously from a workspace root `FolderNode` down to a deeply nested\n`BlockNode` using BFS tool calls to expand subtrees\"* — has been named since early design but\nnever built. This doc is its first concrete implementation: a CLI command that walks the\nfractal tree from any root, showing folded (title-level) structure, bounded by an optional\ndepth.",
    "title": "Aperas-agentic-bfs-projection-design.md",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T04:57:23.177Z",
    "artifactId": "00C66ZNX2R000",
    "fileHash": "d245f6cef735078819e91ed395c5875260538176d73fc2ab4348b06b7bf4548f",
    "ingestedHash": "d245f6cef735078819e91ed395c5875260538176d73fc2ab4348b06b7bf4548f",
    "lastIngestedAt": "2026-08-30T04:23:55.903Z",
    "lastTrackedAt": "2026-08-30T04:23:52.716Z",
    "path": "Aperas-agentic-bfs-projection-design.md",
    "root": "BlockNode/00C66ZPNZG000"
  },
  {
    "@id": "ArtifactNode/00C67F0RS0000",
    "@type": "ArtifactNode",
    "text": "`Aperas-agentic-bfs-projection-design.md` conflated two different traversal shapes under one\nwrong name — a depth-bounded recursive walk it called \"BFS Traversal\" is actually depth-first\n(IDDFS-shaped, once bounded), not breadth-first. This doc replaces it: three distinct tools,\ncorrectly named, plus a third capability (keyword/regex search) every earlier revision flagged\nas missing but never built. Together they answer \"what does an agent or human have to find a\nnode or explore the graph,\" per `Aperas-core-ontology-design.md` §5.A's Agentic Interface\nconcept and the gap first identified in `Aperas-basic-assertion-skill-design.md` §2.",
    "title": "Aperas-agentic-query-tools-design.md",
    "artifactId": "00C67F0RS0000",
    "fileHash": "e1b76d5ca167306af972ddc9c54cdead32d56f8ddaa62070353eb41bda88de8d",
    "ingestedHash": "e1b76d5ca167306af972ddc9c54cdead32d56f8ddaa62070353eb41bda88de8d",
    "lastIngestedAt": "2026-08-30T05:41:17.722Z",
    "lastTrackedAt": "2026-08-30T05:41:14.274Z",
    "path": "Aperas-agentic-query-tools-design.md",
    "root": "BlockNode/00C67F1GER000"
  },
  {
    "@id": "ArtifactNode/00C6HQ9K68000",
    "@type": "ArtifactNode",
    "text": "Markdown/mdast is a **loose** structure: node shapes vary per type (a `heading` carries its\ncontent differently from a `paragraph`; `list`/`listItem` nest oddly; whole categories of block\nexist that the parser doesn't even recognize without extra plugins), and remark itself parses\nheadings as flat siblings rather than a nesting construct. `BlockNode` is meant to be a **tight**,\nuniform, infinitely-recursive structure — the same three-part shape at every depth, described by\n`Aperas-core-ontology-design.md` §5's \"Abstraction by Folding\": *\"an infinite subtree folds into\nan abstract (`text`), which folds into a semantic label (`title`), which folds into a hidden\n`@id`.\"* That's a claim of **uniformity** — every node, regardless of its mdast origin, is\nsupposed to look the same shape-wise. In practice, the mapping in `astParser.ts` has grown\nad hoc, one exception at a time (heading gets special title handling, containers get emptied\ntext, `ordered`/`start`/`checked` bolted on for `list`/`listItem` specifically). This doc is the\nsettled mapping: what `title`/`text`/`children` mean for every block type, uniformly, and an\nhonest audit of what's covered today versus silently isn't.",
    "title": "Aperas-markdown-fractal-mapping-design.md",
    "artifactId": "00C6HQ9K68000",
    "fileHash": "fc1ba3c870a581d666eae298ddfa815e76b14521d925415b1bad7e9c74aed3a3",
    "ingestedHash": "fc1ba3c870a581d666eae298ddfa815e76b14521d925415b1bad7e9c74aed3a3",
    "lastIngestedAt": "2026-08-30T23:23:16.767Z",
    "lastTrackedAt": "2026-08-30T23:23:13.026Z",
    "path": "Aperas-markdown-fractal-mapping-design.md",
    "root": "BlockNode/00C6HQBE0R000"
  },
